bc.. I'm afraid most of my work might have been in vain, as I've come up with an entirely
different approach that should make things even faster and also much cleaner. The
idea is to get rid of the Add class in its present form and instead use a dict to
hold a linear combination with rational coefficients.

This speeds things up tremendously by eliminating allocation and comparison of small
Mul objects within sums. Mul itself could be implemented analogously (dict of
base:exp pairs), to eliminate the need for Pows.

According to a very preliminary timing, this makes small-polynomial addition 50x
faster than in the current SymPy core, even in the case of a trivial (two-term) sum
where storing the unit coefficients explicitly is redundant. This is nearly as fast
as looking up a cached result in the current SymPy core. For the special case of
all-integer coefficients, it could be even faster with proper optimizations. (As
usual, various overheads and special cases will reduce speed, but hopefully not
significantly.)

A great advantage of this approach is that there won't be any need to worry about
placing things in a canonical order, as dict handles that automatically. For
user-friendliness, terms could still be sorted in **repr**, but since it won't be
performance-critical, the sorting could be much more intelligent, e.g. sorting
polynomial terms by degree.

There's obviously the catch that noncommutative objects won't be supported; they'd
require a separate implementation. But I think it'd be worth it.

Here's the idea:

class Rational(tuple):
    def **new**(cls, p, q=1):
        # TODO: gcd
        return tuple.**new**(cls, (p, q))
    def **add**(s, t):
        if not isinstance(t, Rational):
            raise NotImplementedError
        a,b = s
        c,d = t
        p = a_d + b_c
        if not p:
            return zero
        q = b*d
        if q != 1:
            a, b = p, q
            while b:
                a, b = b, a % b
            p //= a
            q //= a
        return tuple.**new**(Rational, (p, q))

